00:49:27.000 --> 00:50:10.000
newspaper. It was postmarked from the San Francisco area and featured no address, just the name of the newspaper, followed by the message, please rush to editor. A curious editor glanced at the envelope before opening it. Inside was a double-sided, handwritten letter, riddled with spelling mistakes and grammatical errors. It read, Dear editor, this is the murderer of the two teenagers last Christmas at Lake Herman
报社。它盖有旧金山地区的邮戳，没有地址，只有报纸的名字，后面附有留言：请速交编辑。一位好奇的编辑在打开信封前瞥了一眼。里面是一封双面手写的信，充满了拼写错误和语法错误。信中写道：尊敬的编辑，我是去年圣诞节在赫尔曼湖杀害那两个青少年的凶手

73
00:50:10.000 --> 00:51:07.000
and the girl on the 4th of July near the golf course in Vallejo. To prove I killed them, I shall state some facts which only I and the police know. At Christmas, one, brand name of ammo, Super X, two, ten shots were fired, three, the boy was on his back with his feet to the car, four, the girl was on her right side, feet to the west, fourth of July, one, girl was wearing patterned slacks, two, the boy was also shot in the knee, three, brand name of ammo was Weston. Here is part of a cipher. The other two parts of this cipher are being mailed to the editors of the Vallejo Times
以及7月4日在巴列佐高尔夫球场附近的女孩。为了证明是我杀了他们，我将说出一些只有我和警察知道的事实。圣诞节，第一，弹药品牌，超级X（Super X），第二，开了十枪，第三，男孩仰面朝天，脚朝向汽车，第四，女孩右侧卧，脚朝西；7月4日，第一，女孩穿着印花休闲裤，第二，男孩的膝盖也被枪击了，第三，弹药品牌是韦斯顿（Weston）。这是一部分密码。这组密码另外两部分正寄给巴列佐时报

74
00:51:07.000 --> 00:51:50.000
and San Francisco Examiner. I want you to print this cipher on the front page of your paper. In this cipher is my identity. If you do not print this cipher by the afternoon of Friday 1st August 69, I will go on a kill rampage Friday night. I will cruise around all weekend killing lone people in the night, then move on to kill again until I end up with a dozen people over the weekend. Beneath this final line was a hand-drawn illustration of a crosshair circle similar to a gun sight.
和旧金山考官报的编辑。我要你们把这组密码印在你们报纸的头版。这组密码里有我的身份。如果你们不在69年8月1日星期五下午之前打印这组密码，我将在周五晚上开始疯狂杀戮。我整个周末都会开车四处游荡，在夜里杀害落单的人，然后继续杀人，直到这个周末结束时我有十几个人命在手。在这最后一行下面是一幅手绘的十字准线圆圈插图，类似于枪的瞄准具。

75
00:51:50.000 --> 00:52:41.000
It appeared the author was taking credit for the December 1968 murders of David Faraday and Betty Lou Jensen as well as the more recent attack against Michael Majo and Darlene Farron. The cipher he referred to was drawn on a smaller sheet of paper. It displayed a series of 136 characters organised into eight neat rows and 17 columns. They ranged from letters of the alphabet to less familiar symbols and shapes such as mathematical signs, naval figures and the same crosshair that the author had used to sign off his letter. Across town and in nearby Vallejo, near identical notes arrived at the editorial departments
看来作者在为1968年12月大卫·法拉第和贝蒂·劳·詹森的谋杀案以及最近对迈克尔·马霍和达琳·法伦的袭击邀功。他提到的密码画在较小的纸上。它显示了一系列136个字符，排列成整齐的八行十七列。它们的范围从英文字母到不那么熟悉的符号和形状，如数学符号、海军图形以及作者用来签署信件的同一个十字准线。在镇子那头和附近的巴列佐，几乎一模一样的便条送达了编辑部

76
00:52:41.000 --> 00:53:23.000
of the San Francisco Examiner and the Vallejo Times Herald. They also contained additional portions of the cryptogram. With all three combined, the cipher featured a total of 408 characters which led to it being dubbed the 408. Police examined each letter for latent fingerprints and sent copies of the cipher to the US Navy radio station cryptographic unit. All they could deduce was that the writer may have been involved with the Navy and was possibly formally trained in encryption.
旧金山考官报和巴列佐时报先驱报。它们还包含了密码的额外部分。三者结合，密码共计408个字符，因此被称为“408密码”。警方检查了每封信上的潜在指纹，并将密码副本发送给美国海军电台密码通信部。他们所能推断出的只是，写信人可能与海军有关，并且可能受过正式的密码破译训练。

77
00:53:23.000 --> 00:54:13.000
Editors at all three newspapers debated whether or not to print the cipher as instructed. Some felt the public had a right to know that a murderer was threatening to go on a killing spree. However, they were reluctant to succumb to a criminal's demands or cause unnecessary public distress as the city's headlines were already dominated by other violent crimes and the turbulent Vietnam War. On the other hand, the cipher had a greater chance of being solved if more people could examine it. The editors finally decided to publish the cryptogram but refused to meet the author's demands regarding the deadline or front page spot.
三家报纸的编辑都在争论是否按照指示打印密码。一些人认为公众有权知道一个杀人犯在威胁要进行疯狂杀戮。然而，他们不愿屈服于罪犯的要求或引起不必要的公众恐慌，因为该市的头条新闻已经被其他暴力犯罪和动荡的越南战争所主导。另一方面，如果更多的人能够检查密码，它被破译的机会就会更大。编辑们最终决定发表密码，但拒绝满足作者关于截止日期或头版位置的要求。

78
00:54:13.000 --> 00:54:55.000
The San Francisco Examiner also ran an article pointing out that investigators couldn't be certain of the author's guilt until he disclosed more details about the crimes. On August 4, a three-page letter arrived at the San Francisco Examiner. It began. In answer to your asking for more details about the good times I've had in Vallejo, I shall be very happy to supply even more material. By the way, are the police having a good time with the code? If not, tell them to cheer up.
旧金山考官报还发表了一篇文章指出，在凶手披露更多犯罪细节之前，调查人员无法确信作者的有罪。8月4日，一封三页的信件送达旧金山考官报。信的开头写道：为了回答你们要求提供更多我在巴列佐度过的好时光的细节，我将非常乐意提供更多材料。顺便问一句，警察们对这个密码玩得开心吗？如果没有，告诉他们振作起来。

79
00:54:55.000 --> 00:55:39.000
When they do crack it, they will have me. As the newspaper requested, the author gave more details about the crimes, including some that hadn't been released to the public. He pointed out things that detectives were wrong about, like their assertion to the press that he would have been able to see Betty Lou Jensen's silhouette as she ran across the turnout. Bullshit, he wrote. That area is surrounded by high hills and trees. He corrected them by explaining that he'd actually attached a small flashlight to his gun in order to see his targets better in the dark.
当他们真正破解它时，他们就拥有我了。正如报纸所要求的，作者提供了关于犯罪的更多细节，包括一些未向公众披露的内容。他指出了侦探们弄错的事情，比如他们向媒体断言，当贝蒂·劳·詹森跑过岔道口时，他本能看到她的剪影。胡说八道，他写道。那个地区周围都是高山和树木。他纠正了他们，解释说他实际上在枪上安装了一个小型手电筒，以便在黑暗中更好地看清目标。

80
00:55:39.000 --> 00:56:35.000
He also took responsibility for the disturbing phone call that dispatcher Nancy Slover received at the Vallejo police department, which he described as having some fun with the Vallejo cops. The letter concluded, I was not happy to see that I did not get front page coverage, followed by the same hand-drawn crosshair symbol featured on the previous letter. The San Francisco Examiner published an article about the latest letter and included a lengthy excerpt. Due to the author's self-introduction, the police, media and public finally had a name for the perpetrator. From this point on, he was known as the Zodiac Killer.
他还对巴列佐警察局调度员南希·斯洛弗收到的那个令人不安的电话承担了责任，他将这描述为“和巴列佐警察找点乐子”。信的结尾是：看到我没有得到头版报道，我很不高兴，随之而来的是前一封信上出现过的同一个手绘十字准线符号。旧金山考官报发表了一篇关于这封最新信件的文章，并收录了一段很长的摘录。由于作者的自我介绍，警方、媒体和公众终于给这个暴行者起了一个名字。从这一刻起，他被称为“黄道带杀手”。

81
00:56:35.000 --> 00:57:38.000
Many people seemed to fit the Zodiac characterization in some capacity. A troubled young man named David was reported after his former foster mother realized he'd been in Vallejo for his brother's funeral at the time of David Faraday and Betty Lou Jensen's murders. While there, he'd been gifted a firearm that had belonged to his brother, a .22 caliber rifle. Most notably of all, when David was upset, he would soothe himself by creating cryptograms. Some of these were passed along to detectives for examination, but they deemed David an unlikely suspect. A wife reported her ex-husband as he was known to sign his letters with a crosshair-like symbol. Another suspect came to light after a couple received a letter from a distant relative claiming he was a killer for the mafia and needed help.
许多人似乎在某种程度上符合黄道带杀手的特征。一个名叫大卫的麻烦年轻人被举报，因为他的前养母意识到在大卫·法拉第和贝蒂·劳·詹森被谋杀时，他为了参加他兄弟的葬礼而在巴列佐。在那里，他曾获赠一把属于他兄弟的火器，一支.22口径步枪。最引人注目的是，当大卫心烦意乱时，他会通过创作密码来安慰自己。其中一些被转交给侦探进行检查，但他们认为大卫不太可能是嫌疑人。一名妻子举报了她的前夫，因为他众所周知喜欢用类似十字准线的符号在信件上签名。另一个嫌疑人浮出水面，是一对夫妇收到了一位远亲的信，声称他是黑手党杀手，需要帮助。

82
00:57:38.000 --> 00:58:43.000
The letter was sent for handwriting analysis with fingerprints lifted and compared to the partial prints on file. He was ruled out of the investigation. Many suspects were interviewed simply because those who reported them thought they had acted strangely and or they owned a gun. While each person varied in intrigue, the actual killer alluded investigators. 41-year-old Donald Hardin was a history and economics teacher from the Californian city of Salinas. In his spare time, he liked to create amateur cryptograms. He and his wife Betty had seen the Zodiac killer's 408 cipher published in the newspapers and decided to try to solve it. Their first move was to figure out which symbol might represent the letter E, the most commonly used letter in the English language.
这封信被送去进行笔迹分析，提取了指纹并与档案中的部分指纹进行了比对。他在调查中被排除。许多嫌疑人接受讯问仅仅是因为举报他们的人认为他们表现怪异和/或他们拥有枪支。虽然每个人引发的兴趣各不相同，但真正的凶手却避开了调查人员。41岁的唐纳德·哈丁是加利福尼亚州萨利纳斯市的一名历史和经济学教师。在业余时间，他喜欢创作业余密码。他和他的妻子贝蒂在报纸上看到了黄道带杀手的408密码，决定尝试破解它。他们的第一步是弄清楚哪个符号可能代表字母E，这是英文字母中最常用的字母。

83
00:58:43.000 --> 00:59:38.000
However, the killer had apparently anticipated this approach and deliberately used 12 different symbols to signify E to thwart would-be code breakers. Donald and Betty considered the matter for a while, then tried a different tack. They figured the author had likely used the word kill more than once, so they began looking for four-letter patterns that would fit with this. For 20 hours, they painstakingly chipped away at the code. Finally, they cracked it. The decoded message read, I like killing people because it is so much fun. It is more fun than killing wild game in the forest because man is the most dangerous animal of all.
然而，凶手显然预料到了这种方法，并故意使用了12个不同的符号来表示E，以阻挠未来的密码破译者。唐纳德和贝蒂考虑了一会儿这个问题，然后尝试了不同的策略。他们认为作者很可能不止一次使用过“杀戮”（kill）这个词，于是开始寻找适合这个词的四个字母的模式。在20个小时里，他们煞费苦心地攻克这个密码。最终，他们破解了。解码后的信息写道：我喜欢杀人，因为这太有趣了。这比在森林里猎杀野生动物更有趣，因为人类是所有动物中最危险的。

84
00:59:38.000 --> 01:00:44.000
To kill something gives me the most thrilling experience. It is even better than getting your rocks off with a girl. The best part of it is that when I die, I will be reborn in paradise and all the people I have killed will become my slaves. I will not give you my name because you will try to slow down or stop my collecting of slaves for my afterlife. Following this was a garbled string of 18 letters that couldn't be deciphered using the code. Donald suspected that a separate method would be needed to unravel this final sentence and that it might hold the key to the zodiac's identity. He sent the decoded message to the San Francisco Chronicle along with several sheets of paper that he'd scribbled his work notes on. The newspaper's editors received the documents on August 8 and immediately notified Vallejo Detective Sergeant John Lynch.
杀戮给我最惊险的体验。这甚至比和一个女孩做爱还要好。最棒的部分是，当我死后，我在乐园里会获得新生，我杀过所有人都会成为我的奴隶。我不会告诉你我的名字，因为你会试图减慢或阻止我为来世收集奴隶。在这之后是一串乱码，共18个字母，无法用该密码解密。唐纳德怀疑需要一种单独的方法来解开这个最后的句子，并且它可能掌握着黄道带身份的钥匙。他将解码后的信息以及几张写满工作笔记的纸张一起发送给旧金山纪事报。报社编辑于8月8日收到文件，并立即通知了巴列佐侦探中士约翰·林奇。

85
01:00:44.000 --> 01:01:53.000
Detective Lynch raced up to San Francisco to collect the letter and worksheets. As he cast his eyes over the decoded message, he was hit by a wave of disappointment. He'd hoped it would contain specific information about the killer's identity. The message was also sent to the President of the American Cryptogram Association who agreed with Donald's translation. To him, it was clear that the person who had created the cipher was extremely adept at designing codes. The biggest clue in the message was the killer's description of humans as the most dangerous animal of all. This was likely a reference to a short story titled The Most Dangerous Game in which a character kills people for sport after growing bored with hunting animals. The decoded cipher was published and people soon started coming forward claiming they could help decipher the garbled text at the end.
林奇侦探赶到旧金山去收集信件和工作表。当他把目光投向解码后的信息时，一阵失望袭来。他曾希望其中能包含关于凶手身份的具体信息。这条信息还被发送给美国密码协会会长，他赞同唐纳德的翻译。对他来说，很明显创造这个密码的人非常擅长设计密码。消息中最大的线索是凶手将人类描述为所有动物中最危险的。这很可能暗指一篇名为《最危险的游戏》的短篇小说，其中一个角色在对猎杀动物感到厌烦后为了消遣而杀人。解码后的密码被公开发表，人们很快开始站出来声称他们可以帮忙破译末尾的乱码。

86
01:01:53.000 --> 01:02:56.000
One thought it might read, Robert Emmett, the hippie. Another believed it could be a coded phone number. One tipster informed the police that the killer's reference to accumulating slaves for the afterlife was a spiritual concept found in parts of the Philippines and that the zodiac may have a connection to the area. All of these theories were investigated, but nothing came of them. Detectives were no closer to identifying a suspect and fears grew that the zodiac could soon strike again. Almost three months later, rookie officer David Slate was manning the telephone switchboard of the Napa Police Department when a call came through at 7.40pm. A male voice was on the other line. In a calm tone, he stated,
一个人认为它可能读作：罗伯特·埃米特，嬉皮士。另一个人认为它可能是一个编码的电话号码。一个线人通知警方，凶手提到为来世积聚奴隶是菲律宾部分地区发现的一种精神概念，黄道带可能与该地区有联系。所有这些理论都得到了调查，但没有得出任何结果。侦探们距离识别嫌疑人并不更近，人们越来越担心黄道带可能很快会再次发动袭击。近三个月后，菜鸟警官大卫·斯莱特正在纳帕警察局的电话总机值班，晚上7点40分左右一个电话打了进来。电话那头是一个男人的声音。他以平静的语气说道，

87
01:02:56.000 --> 01:03:01.000
I want to report a murder. No, a double murder. They are two miles north of Park Headquarters. They were in a white Volkswagen Karman gear. Officer Slate thought the caller sounded young as though he was in his early twenties. Trying to coax out more information, the officer asked the man where he was. In a quiet, barely audible voice, the caller simply replied, I'm the one who did it. To be continued next week. There aren't comments yet for this episode. Click on any sentence in the transcript to leave a comment.
我要报告一起谋杀案。不，是一起双重谋杀。它们在公园总部以北两英里处。它们在一辆白色的大众卡门吉亚（Volkswagen Karmann Ghia）里。斯莱特警官认为打电话的人听起来很年轻，好像二十岁出头。为了诱导更多信息，警官问那个男人在哪里。在平静、几乎听不见的声音中，打电话的人简单地回答：我就是干的。下周继续。这集还没有评论。点击剧本中的任意句子来留下评论。
